<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Screenshot-only service, deliberately minimal.

     What is left here is the smallest declaration that still takes a screenshot
     inside the app: the screenshot capability, no window content, no key events,
     no gestures, no event types. Everything else this service used to declare was
     removed one at a time to test whether it was what bank/UPI scanners read, and
     every one of those tests came back negative:

       1.1.9  canTakeScreenshot removed            -> still flagged
       1.1.12 accessibilityEventTypes removed      -> still flagged
       1.1.13 canTakeScreenshot removed (both)     -> still flagged
       1.1.14 mediaProjection declaration removed  -> still flagged
       1.1.16 isAccessibilityTool="true" added     -> still flagged

     1.1.16 is the reason the flag is gone. It was set at the owner's request
     against the expectation, written down beforehand, that it would change
     nothing — and it changed nothing. It was also a false declaration: SnapTile
     is a screenshot tool anyone can use, not a service that exists to assist
     users with disabilities, which is what the flag asserts, and Google's own
     guidance says declaring it without that purpose is what triggers a Play
     Protect warning naming the app. A false claim that also bought nothing does
     not belong in the tree.

     canTakeScreenshot came back in 1.2.0, and the reason is the matrix above: it
     was tested in both directions, and the answer was identical both times, so it
     is free. With it declared, takeScreenshot() returns the bitmap in-app, which
     is what makes region and paint modes work without a consent dialog. A build
     without it still captures: the service detects the missing capability at
     request time and routes accordingly (see
     ScreenshotAccessibilityService.hasScreenshotCapability()).

     The external check on all of this is ScreenshotTile
     (github.com/cvzi/ScreenshotTile), which Super Money flags too. Its
     accessibility config declares one event type and a description and no
     capability at all — less than anything SnapTile has ever shipped — and it is
     flagged anyway. So the trigger is not a field in this file. It is the app
     presenting itself as a screenshot tool with an accessibility service enabled,
     which is a property of this whole category of app, not of a configuration.

     One trap when reading the built APK: this attribute set only exists in the
     -v22 resource variant. aapt2 strips attributes newer than minSdk (21) out of
     the base res/xml file and writes them to res/xml-v22/, so dumping the base
     variant shows seven attributes and looks exactly like the capability and
     flags were silently dropped. An API 36 device reads the versioned variant.
     Check res/xml-v22/ before concluding anything about this file. -->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:canRequestTouchExplorationMode="false"
    android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="false"
    android:canPerformGestures="false"
    android:canTakeScreenshot="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_desc"
    android:summary="@string/accessibility_desc" />
